Here's how to implement all three requirements:

## 1. Intercept Back Navigation with a Confirmation Dialog

Override `OnBackButtonPressed` in your page:

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
            return base.OnBackButtonPressed();

        // Fire-and-forget the async dialog; return true to cancel navigation
        Dispatcher.Dispatch(async () =>
        {
            bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                "Unsaved Changes",
                "You have unsaved changes. Discard them?",
                "Discard", "Stay");

            if (leave)
            {
                _hasUnsavedChanges = false;
                await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            }
        });

        return true; // cancel the default back navigation
    }
}
```

> **Note:** `OnBackButtonPressed` is synchronous, so you must return `true` immediately to block navigation, then show the async dialog via `Dispatcher.Dispatch`.

## 2. Custom "Cancel" Back Button in the Toolbar

In your XAML, hide the default back button and add your own:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.EditProductPage"
             Shell.BackButtonBehavior="{StaticResource CancelBackButton}">

    <ContentPage.Resources>
        <BackButtonBehavior x:Key="CancelBackButton"
                            TextOverride="Cancel"
                            Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" />
    </ContentPage.Resources>

    <!-- Or inline: -->
    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior TextOverride="Cancel"
                            Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

Then in your view model (or code-behind):

```csharp
public ICommand CancelCommand { get; }

public EditProductPage()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    CancelCommand = new Command(async () =>
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            return;
        }

        bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard them?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (leave)
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    });
    BindingContext = this;
}
```

## Key Points

| Concern | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Android/hardware back | `OnBackButtonPressed()` override |
| Shell toolbar back button | `BackButtonBehavior` with `Command` |
| Custom label | `TextOverride="Cancel"` |
| Hide default icon | `IconOverride=""` (empty string) |

Both paths must be handled because `BackButtonBehavior.Command` **does not** trigger `OnBackButtonPressed`, and vice versa. Keep your confirmation logic in a shared method to avoid duplication.
